ative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source supports the ordered progression from study in 1938, to designation authorization in 1944, to construction funding in 1956.","materiality":"Establishes the central sequence and differentiates the acts’ functions."},{"excerpt":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the chief of the Bureau of Public Roads to study a proposed six-route toll network.","claim":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrative":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The claim matches the source; changing the possessive phrasing to “chief of” does not alter the role or action.","materiality":"Identifies the first legislative step and its limited subject."},{"excerpt":"The 1944 Act then authorized the designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","claim":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrative":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The act authorized designation; the piece does not imply that the miles were completed or constructed.","materiality":"The mileage and authorization distin